`ÔDCTÁ¨ˆ	ƒTCQ¨†¢ &S¢ "¦D@D5Lˆ€˜5Gu0j˜5Õ0j"!ª`ÔDCTÁ¨ˆ†©ƒQS¢ "¦D@D5Lˆ€ˆjˆj"mÕÔDCTÁ¨ˆ†¨†¢ "¢ˆ€˜5D5{PŞ^†9ñ—Gä?èV¨‹ˆoD³ÿÙ# Web-client overrides, preloaded into /data/webdefaults and pointed to by
# Module.arguments' --userconfigdir (web/shell.html) and web/Makefile's
# `--preload-file web/webdefaults@/data/webdefaults`.
#
# This is defence in depth, not the primary guard: the actual risk -- WebGL's
# GL_VENDOR string tripping rScreen.cpp's NVIDIA sniff and turning on
# INFINITY_PLANE / USE_DISPLAYLISTS -- was removed at the source in Task 5.
# Do not describe this file as what keeps display lists off; that patch is.
#
# The guard on that branch is
#     #if defined( DEDICATED ) || !defined( __EMSCRIPTEN__ )
# and NOT the bare #ifndef __EMSCRIPTEN__ an earlier version of this comment
# claimed. The difference is load-bearing, not pedantry: em++ defines
# __EMSCRIPTEN__ for BOTH wasm builds this repo produces, so a bare #ifndef
# would have deleted the branch from the M0 dedicated server as well and
# changed its wasm -- the one thing every task on this branch had to keep
# byte-identical at 2,488,298 bytes AND md5 9718a2a64978cb6e9b95ea2f0454cca5.
# Quote both: M4 task 3 measured an unguarded edit linking to exactly
# 2,488,298 bytes with a different md5, so the size alone does not catch it.
# See docs/porting/browser-runtime-notes.md
# section 1 for the guard table, and for why you cannot settle a question
# like this by eye or with an #ifdef-counting script: use em++ -E.
#
# WHY "autoexec.cfg" AND NOT "settings_web.cfg": an earlier draft of this
# file used a made-up name, which nothing in the source ever opens by that
# literal string -- inert. "autoexec.cfg" is not made up: it is one of the
# handful of filenames st_LoadConfig() (src/tools/tConfiguration.cpp:992)
# opens unconditionally, in BOTH the client and dedicated build, no
# #ifdef/first-use gate. It is found here because /data/webdefaults is
# already in tPathConfig's search list (src/tools/tDirectories.cpp:500-515)
# whenever --userconfigdir is passed, and tPath::Open (tDirectories.cpp:669)
# iterates that list highest-priority-first and returns on the first file it
# finds -- so as long as no OTHER autoexec.cfg exists anywhere else in the
# bundle to be found first (none does: this is the only one shipped), this
# file loads. It loads AFTER both user.cfg and settings.cfg
# (tConfiguration.cpp's load order), so its values win over both.
#
# A rejected alternative, recorded so it isn't tried again: renaming this to
# "default.cfg" instead. default.cfg only loads while st_FirstUse is true
# (tConfiguration.cpp:984), i.e. before M4 persists a user.cfg -- so it would
# stop taking effect the moment persistence lands, silently. Worse, it would
# also entirely SHADOW the shipped /data/config/default.cfg (same filename,
# higher-priority search path), losing SERVER_NAME, the tooltip defaults and
# everything else in it, not just adding to them. autoexec.cfg has neither
# problem: it isn't gated on first-use, and there is no shipped
# /data/config/autoexec.cfg for it to collide with.
#
# TWO THINGS FOR M2/M4 TO KNOW ABOUT THIS MECHANISM, not specific to the
# settings below:
#
# 1. /data/webdefaults REPLACES, it does not MERGE. tPath::Open returns the
#    first whole file it finds and stops looking -- it does not combine a
#    same-named file from two directories line by line. Anything else
#    dropped into web/webdefaults/ that happens to share a filename with a
#    file already under config/ (a settings.cfg, a keys_wasd.cfg) would
#    silently REPLACE the shipped one in full, not extend it. This file is
#    named autoexec.cfg specifically because nothing under config/ is named
#    that, so there is nothing to shadow.
#
# 2. Loading after user.cfg makes every setting below a HARD OVERRIDE, not a
#    default -- the opposite of what "webdefaults" as a directory name
#    suggests. THIS IS THE TEST FOR WHETHER A SETTING BELONGS IN THIS FILE AT
#    ALL. Ask: may the player change it and keep the change?
#
#      * NO, it would break the port -> it belongs here. USE_DISPLAYLISTS and
#        INFINITY_PLANE are exactly that: never want them on, full stop,
#        regardless of what a user's saved config says.
#      * The item is a tSettingItem, so it is read from config but never
#        written back to user.cfg -> it belongs here too, harmlessly, because
#        there is no player choice for it to overwrite. CONSOLE_LADDER_LOG
#        (ePlayer.cpp) and SP_LIMIT_ROUNDS (gGame.cpp) are in this class.
#      * YES, it is a preference -> it does NOT belong here. Put it in the
#        binary as a compiled default under
#            #if !defined( DEDICATED ) && defined( __EMSCRIPTEN__ )
#        Then it applies when there is no user.cfg and loses to user.cfg the
#        moment there is one.
#
#    M4 TASK 3 MOVED THE TWO SETTINGS THAT FAILED THAT TEST, and moved their
#    reasoning with them -- follow the comment, it did not evaporate:
#      MAX_FPS 60           -> sr_maxFPS in src/render/rSysdep.cpp
#      SOUND_BUFFER_SHIFT 1 -> buffer_shift in src/engine/eSound.cpp, together
#                              with M3's whole measured latency/starvation
#                              table, which is the evidence for the value.
#    Both are menu-reachable (Display Settings / Screen Mode, and the Sound
#    menu) and both are tConfItem, so both really were saved by the player and
#    really were reverted on the next load. Re-ordering this file's load could
#    not have fixed it: user.cfg's load is already the FIRST one st_LoadConfig
#    performs, so there is no slot to move ahead of it.
#
#    STILL UNRESOLVED, and deliberately so: SP_NUM_AIS and SP_AUTO_AIS below
#    are tConfItem (gGame.cpp), and gGameSettings::Menu() is reachable on the
#    singlePlayer object via GameSettingsSP(), so they have the same defect --
#    a player who changes the opponent count keeps it only until the next
#    load. They are left as hard overrides on purpose because they are what
#    pins the M2/M3 gate to "three AIs" regardless of the profile a run starts
#    from; a gate whose opponent count depended on a saved config would not be
#    a fixed experiment. If the SP_* settings ever stop being gate scaffolding
#    they should move too.
#
# MAX_FPS IS NOT SET HERE ANY MORE -- do not add it back. It was `MAX_FPS 60`
# until M4 task 3; it is now the compiled default of sr_maxFPS in
# src/render/rSysdep.cpp, so a player can change it in Display Settings /
# Screen Mode and keep the change. Setting it here again would restore the bug
# that move fixed, silently. Note it is genuinely LIVE either way: the frame
# limiter it drives, sr_LimitFPS(), sleeps via emscripten_sleep() under
# Emscripten instead of SDL_Delay(). (An earlier revision of this file claimed
# MAX_FPS had no effect in the browser, because the limiter had been compiled
# out on the theory that a second Asyncify yield per frame was what broke the
# client. That diagnosis was wrong -- see
# docs/porting/browser-runtime-notes.md section 8.)
#
# INFINITY_PLANE and USE_DISPLAYLISTS STAY. They are the "no, it would break
# the port" case in the test above: correctness settings, not preferences, and
# a saved user.cfg must not be able to turn them on.
INFINITY_PLANE 0
USE_DISPLAYLISTS 0

# SOUND_BUFFER_SHIFT IS NOT SET HERE ANY MORE -- do not add it back. It was
# `SOUND_BUFFER_SHIFT 1` from M3 until M4 task 3; it is now the compiled
# default of buffer_shift in src/engine/eSound.cpp, so a player can change the
# audio buffer size in the Sound menu and keep the change.
#
# M3'S MEASURED TABLE WENT WITH IT AND IS NOT LOST. The comment on buffer_shift
# in src/engine/eSound.cpp carries all of it: the frames-per-shift table, the
# four-row latency/starvation measurement from three full rounds in Chrome, the
# argument that latency and starvation tolerance are the same quantity because
# Emscripten queues 0.05 s + 5 x bufferDuration ahead of the clock, the margin
# comparison that picked 1 over 0 (2.0-2.4x against 1.1-1.4x on a 119-142 ms
# worst main-thread stall), why upstream's non-WIN32 default of 0 does not
# transfer to a single-threaded device, and why 3 was abandoned. Read it there
# before changing the value -- and revisit with evidence, not taste.

# CONSOLE_LADDER_LOG is the only instrumentation this port has for "did a round
# actually start?". se_SaveToLadderLog (src/engine/ePlayer.cpp:6179) writes the
# ladder-log events to std::cout -- which is the browser console under
# Emscripten -- prefixed "[L] ", but ONLY when this is set; otherwise they go
# only to ladderlog.txt, in an in-memory var directory nothing ever reads back.
# With it on, entering a round announces itself as "[L] NEW_ROUND"
# (src/tron/gGame.cpp:3130), which is what the M2 gate greps for. It costs one
# console line per game event and nothing else.
#
# It is usable client-side despite the server-sounding name: the tSettingItem is
# compiled into both builds, and only the ladderlog.txt half of
# se_SaveToLadderLog is gated on sn_GetNetState()!=nCLIENT -- the std::cout half
# is not, so it fires in a local single-player game too.
CONSOLE_LADDER_LOG 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The M2 gate settings: three rounds against three AI opponents.
#
# All three are SP_-prefixed because the only game this client can start today
# is a LOCAL SINGLE-PLAYER one, and gGame.cpp keeps two entirely separate
# gGameSettings objects (gGame.cpp:518-531): `singlePlayer`, which the SP_*
# items write, and `multiPlayer`, which the unprefixed NUM_AIS / AUTO_AIS /
# LIMIT_ROUNDS items write. sg_currentSettings points at `singlePlayer`
# whenever sg_singlePlayer is true (gGame.cpp:1447-1450). Setting the
# unprefixed names here would parse cleanly, take effect on the wrong object
# and change nothing observable -- the same shape of silent no-op this file's
# header comment already records for a made-up filename.
#
# SP_NUM_AIS 3      -- gGame.cpp:576, tConfItem<int>  -> singlePlayer.numAIs.
#                      Ships as 1 (gGame.cpp:518, the fifth constructor
#                      argument), so a default run faces ONE opponent.
#                      Consumed by gAIPlayer::SetNumberOfAIs (called from
#                      gGame.cpp:1474, defined at gAIBase.cpp:1164), which adds
#                      or removes AI players at the start of every round until
#                      the count matches. Capped at MAXAI, the aiplayers.cfg
#                      roster size (22 characters shipped), so 3 is in range.
#
# SP_AUTO_AIS 0     -- gGame.cpp:580, tConfItem<bool> -> singlePlayer.autoNum.
#                      Ships as TRUE. With it on, gGameSettings::AutoAI
#                      (gGame.cpp:223-247) moves numAIs up or down after every
#                      round depending on whether the human won, so "three AIs"
#                      would be a property of the first round only. Turning it
#                      off is what makes the opponent count a fixed property of
#                      the run rather than a function of how the player did.
#
#                      For the FIRST match it is belt and braces: welcome()
#                      (gArmagetron.cpp:378-395) saves autoNum, forces it to 0
#                      for the tutorial game and restores it afterwards. This
#                      setting is what keeps the count fixed for every match
#                      after that one.
#
# SP_LIMIT_ROUNDS 3 -- gGame.cpp:573, tSettingItem<int> ->
#                      singlePlayer.limitRounds. Ships as 10. tSettingItem is a
#                      tConfItem subclass whose only difference is Save()
#                      returning false (tConfiguration.h:484-500): it is read
#                      from a config file exactly like a tConfItem and is
#                      merely never written back to user.cfg. So setting it
#                      here works, and keeps working when M4 adds persistence.
#
# Deliberately NOT set here: FIRST_USE 0. It reaches the main menu directly and
# drops the per-frame glFinish() that sr_LoadDefaultConfig installs
# (rScreen.cpp:1029) -- but it also skips sg_StartupPlayerMenu(), the only
# caller that loads any keys_*.cfg, leaving a menu with no key bindings at all.
# The gate drives the first-run setup flow instead, which is also what a real
# first-time visitor sees.
SP_NUM_AIS 3
SP_AUTO_AIS 0
SP_LIMIT_ROUNDS 3
